{
 "id": "zig",
 "name": "Zig",
 "aliases": ["Ziglang"],
 "categories": ["general-purpose", "systems"],
 "paradigms": ["imperative", "procedural", "generic", "concurrent"],
 "typing": ["static", "strong", "nominal", "inferred-local", "manifest-comptime-generics"],
 "first_appeared": 2016,
 "designers": ["Andrew Kelley"],
 "summary": "Low-level systems language positioned as a modern replacement for C: no hidden control flow or hidden allocations, explicit allocators, error unions with try/catch/errdefer, optionals instead of null pointers, and compile-time execution (comptime) used for generics and reflection. The zig toolchain doubles as a C/C++ cross compiler.",
 "status": "active",
 "defined_by": ["reference-implementation", "vendor-specification"],
 "file_extensions": [".zig", ".zon"],
 "homepage": "https://ziglang.org/",
 "influenced_by": ["c", "cpp", "rust", "go"],
 "related": [{"id": "c", "relation": "family"}, {"id": "c3", "relation": "family"}, {"id": "odin", "relation": "family"}, {"id": "hare", "relation": "family"}],
 "level_notes": {"0": "The self-hosted compiler (Zig, with LLVM, C and native x86-64/aarch64/wasm/... backends) is the reference implementation. Development moved from GitHub to Codeberg (codeberg.org/ziglang/zig) in late 2025; github.com/ziglang/zig is the archived former home. Codeberg serves anti-scraper pages to automated clients, so the Codeberg file links were verified against the repository tree from the Codeberg API rather than by the link checker. Pipeline: tokenizer -> Parse (AST) -> AstGen (ZIR) -> Sema (AIR, comptime evaluation) -> codegen/link.", "1": "No standard and no complete formal specification: the Language Reference (one HTML page per release, linked here for 0.17.1) is the de-facto definition together with the compiler; the zig-spec repository holds an unfinished draft spec and the PEG grammar. Language changes are proposals filed as issues in the issue tracker; release notes document each language change. Zig is pre-1.0 and breaks compatibility between minor releases.", "2": "zig.guide, 'Learning Zig' and the open Zig book track recent releases but may lag the newest language changes."},
